---
name: kimi-task
description: Delegate a coding task to the Kimi Code CLI, then report its output and any file changes
argument-hint: '[--background] [--continue] [-m <model>] <task>'
disable-model-invocation: true
allowed-tools: Bash(kimi:*), Bash(git:*)
---

Delegate a task to the Kimi Code CLI (`kimi`) and report back.

Raw slash-command arguments:
`$ARGUMENTS`

Core constraints:
- You are a thin dispatcher. Kimi does the work — do not implement, extend, or fix anything yourself.
- Return Kimi's output faithfully, and leave Kimi's changes alone unless the user asks you to touch them.

Argument handling:
- `--background`: run in a background Bash task.
- `--continue`: resume Kimi's most recent session in this directory (pass `--continue` through to kimi).
- `-m <model>`: pass through to kimi (e.g. `-m kimi-code/kimi-for-coding`).
- Everything else is the task description.

Compose the prompt:
- One self-contained task per run: state the goal, the relevant constraints, and an explicit definition of done.
- Preserve the user's wording and intent; add repository context (paths, commands) only when Kimi needs it to start working immediately.
- When resuming with `--continue`, write a delta instruction ("Continue from where you left off: ...") rather than restating the whole original task.

Run from the project root. Note: headless Kimi auto-approves its own tool calls — it can and will edit files.

Foreground:
```bash
kimi -p "<composed task prompt>" [--continue] [-m <model>]
```

Background: the same command via Bash with `run_in_background: true` when `--background` was passed or the task is clearly long (multi-file feature, large refactor). Tell the user it is running; the result will arrive as a task notification.

Report:
- Present Kimi's stdout verbatim first.
- Then show what changed: `git status --short --untracked-files=all` and `git diff --stat` (skip silently outside a git repo).
- Do not review, refactor, or "clean up" Kimi's work unprompted.

Failures:
- `kimi` not found: point the user at `/kimi-setup`.
- stderr mentions login, 401, unauthorized, or API key: tell the user to type `! kimi login` or run `/kimi-setup`.
- Anything else: report the exit code and the relevant stderr lines verbatim.
